ors that our  targets were indeed expressed at that  time and so we don't know but we have a  high degree of confidence that they were  too deep there okay and then you'd  mentioned that there was a difference  between donors potentially due to a snip  and you show some good mapping there did  you essentially resolve that just by  having the multiple different CRISPR  designs that you were able to you know  maybe maybe discard the one that was  showing a big difference and look at the  others or how did you resolve that yeah  isn't that cool as you know working with  all these different donor cells it just  you know it really blew my mind how  different you know each donor to donor  was and these these snips created large  disagreements in editing efficiency but  luckily you know that's why we designed  three guides per gene not only because  it's hard to predict which one is going  to be most efficient but also to just in  case we accidentally hit one of these  snips and then I showed you one example  of that but there were actually quite a  few of them but a long thing that gets  it's something that gets discussed but  to actually see the evidence of it you  know makes it makes it very real I know  I was just crazy we couldn't believe it  it was a lot of work getting all the  sequencing data but we were so happy  that we did because we we just learned  so much from it hey I'd highly recommend  that if people are using this technology  either in primary cells or in the cell  line you know  if you can get sequencing data from that  specific line or cell type that you're  using that is way more useful for  designing your guides and aligning it  just to the reference sequence or design  multiple guides like we did here it's  very important right makes sense so with  that let me just wrap up with one just  sort of what what you might want your  parting thoughts to be so obviously you  you have all of the kind of the nuts and  bolts of the workflow written up in that  protocol that will make sure is is made  available to all the attendees but what  would you say is kind of the biggest  consideration or pitfall to avoid in in  really taking on you know this CRISPR  caste 9 editing in primary cells  I think the most important thing to keep  in mind if you're going to be doing gene  editing in primary cells is that you  monitor the efficiency of your knock out  every time that you do it so we found  that donor to donor you don't always get  good correlation genes gene you don't  always get correlation and so being sure  that you validate the reagents that  you're using and the knock out after  you've generated it every time you do it  is really essential to be sure that  you're tracking your phenotype correctly  that was one of our big takeaways from  this work all right excellent well I  will let you end on that then thank you  to the audience for all those great  questions and Judd thank you very much  for the presentation and in all of your  insights in answering those questions  absolutely thank you so much for having  me you bet  I also want to in dition to thanking you  again thanking lab roots and horizon  discovery for underwriting today's  webcast this webcast can be viewed  on-demand through March of next year and  lab roots will send all registered  attendees and email when it's available  to replay on-demand and so we certainly  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event and then following  that it will be hosted as well on the  thermicon segment of the horizon  discovery website so with that until  next time thanks everyone for joining  and have a great day
 

